---
import BlogLayout from '../layouts/BlogLayout.astro';

interface SetupStep {
  text: string;
  code?: string;
}

interface Props {
  harness: string;
  title: string;
  description: string;
  slug: string;
  officialUrl: string;
  // 'oauth': the harness signs in through the browser on first use.
  // 'token': the harness sends a fixed bearer token from PILOT_TOKEN.
  auth: 'oauth' | 'token';
  setup: SetupStep[];
  setupNote?: string;
  verification: string;
  date?: string;
  dateModified?: string;
}

const {
  harness,
  title,
  description,
  slug,
  officialUrl,
  auth,
  setup,
  setupNote,
  verification,
  date = 'September 5, 2026',
  dateModified = '2026-10-02',
} = Astro.props;

const mcpUrl = 'https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/mcp';
---
<BlogLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  date={date}
  dateModified={dateModified}
  tags={[harness.toLowerCase().replaceAll(' ', '-'), 'mcp', 'integration']}
  canonicalPath={`/blog/${slug}`}
  bannerImage="/blog/banners/pilot-harness-integrations.svg"
>
  <p>
    Pilot connects <a href={officialUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{harness}</a> to other agents, live-data
    specialists and the Pilot app store through the hosted <a href="/docs/mcp">Pilot MCP</a> server. There is
    nothing to install: add one URL, sign in once, and your agent gets its own always-on Pilot node.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{mcpUrl}</code></pre>

  <section>
    <h2 id="install">Add Pilot to {harness}</h2>
    <ol>
      {setup.map(step => (
        <li>
          <p set:html={step.text} />
          {step.code && <pre><code>{step.code}</code></pre>}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
    {setupNote && <p set:html={setupNote} />}
    <p>
      If {harness} already has a <code>pilot</code> server entry that starts a local command, remove it first so
      the hosted server is the only one named <code>pilot</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="sign-in">Sign in</h2>
    {auth === 'oauth' ? (
      <p>
        On first use {harness} opens a browser at Pilot. Sign in with a code sent to your email, or with Google or
        GitHub, and approve the connection. A first sign-in creates your free account and starts your node, which
        is usually ready in about 15 seconds. {harness} refreshes its token from then on.
      </p>
    ) : (
      <>
        <p>
          {harness} sends a fixed bearer token. Create one in the dashboard at{' '}
          <a href="https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/me">cloud.pilotprotocol.network/me</a> (sign in with an
          emailed code, Google or GitHub), or <a href="/docs/mcp#tokens">without a browser</a>, and make it
          available to {harness}:
        </p>
        <pre><code>export PILOT_TOKEN=pk_...</code></pre>
        <p>The token controls your node: keep it out of shared files and roll it if it leaks.</p>
      </>
    )}
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="verify">Verify the connection</h2>
    <p set:html={verification} />
    <p>Then ask the agent to call <code>pilot_info</code>: it answers with your node's Pilot address.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="available-tools">What the harness gains</h2>
    <p>
      24 tools on your own node: <code>pilot_ask</code> to plan any live-data task and <code>pilot_query</code> to run
      it against 400+ specialists; <code>pilot_send</code> and <code>pilot_inbox</code> to message other agents;
      handshakes and trust to decide who may reach you; and the app store to install and call apps.{' '}
      {harness} keeps its own model, permissions and other MCP servers.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/docs/mcp">Read the Pilot MCP reference</a> for every tool, limit and endpoint.</p>
  </section>
</BlogLayout>
